  "plain_language_conclusion": "STATISTICAL CONCLUSION ON MODEL ROBUSTNESS:\n----------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. CHANCE AND BASELINE COMPARISON:\n   - On test Macro-F1, K-Nearest Neighbors (point: 0.3107, 95% CI: [0.2423, 0.3811]) overlaps substantially with the 100-seed Stratified Dummy baseline (mean: 0.3285, 95% range: [0.2495, 0.4088]).\n   - On Balanced Accuracy, K-Nearest Neighbors (point: 0.3212, 95% CI: [0.2577, 0.3838]) fails to beat naive chance (0.3333). Its confidence interval fully encompasses the majority-class baseline (0.3333).\n   - On Raw Accuracy, K-Nearest Neighbors (42.67%) is 10 percentage points BELOW the naive majority-class baseline (52.67%).\n\n2. TEMPORAL GENERALIZATION (5-FOLD EXPANDING ROLLING ORIGIN):\n   - Across 5 chronological folds, K-Nearest Neighbors achieved mean Macro-F1 of 0.3114 +/- 0.0247, whereas the Stratified Dummy baseline achieved 0.3352 +/- 0.0491.\n   - KNN failed to consistently outperform naive baselines across time windows.\n\n3. PERMUTATION TEST SIGNIFICANCE:\n   - Permuting the training labels 200 times yielded an empirical p-value of p = 0.5473.\n   - The model result is NOT statistically distinguishable from random noise (p >= 0.05).\n\n4. HIGH-SEVERITY DETECTION FAILURE:\n   - K-Nearest Neighbors detected only 1 of 23 High-severity incidents (4.3% recall).\n   - Its 95% bootstrap confidence interval is [0.0000, 0.1429], confirming that High-severity recall is statistically indistinguishable from zero.\n\nFINAL VERDICT:\nTHERE IS NO STATISTICAL EVIDENCE THAT ANY MODEL BEATS CHANCE OR NAIVE BASELINES IN A OPERATIONALLY VIABLE MANNER. The models suffer from extreme false-negative rates on severe crimes and offer zero reliable predictive signal beyond naive priors."
